      "wordMax": 200,
      "wordMin": 180,
      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nLucy Adeyemi, 4 years 2 months.\nLives with both parents and her brother, aged seven, who has no walking problems.\nStarts reception class at the local primary school in early September.\nReferred by you on 30 July 2026 after parental concern. Seen with both parents, 26 August 2026.\n\nReason for referral:\nParents report that her feet turn inwards when she walks and runs, and that she trips frequently.\nThey have been advised by a relative that she needs corrective boots.\nThe relative's own child wore corrective boots in the 1990s, which the parents found persuasive.\nTheir main worry is that she will fall more often or be teased once she starts school.\nThe parents had already priced corrective boots online but agreed to wait for this assessment.\n\nHistory:\nBorn at term, uncomplicated delivery, birth weight 3.5 kg.\nDevelopment normal throughout; walked independently at thirteen months.\nSpeech and fine motor skills reported as normal by her nursery key worker.\nHeight and weight both on the 50th centile in her personal child health record.\nNo pain at any time. She does not complain and is not limited in play.\nEnjoys dancing and riding her scooter, and keeps up with her brother at the park.\nCan hop on either foot and climbs stairs one foot per step.\nNo family history of orthopaedic or neuromuscular conditions.\nNo regression of any skill.\nHad chickenpox in the spring, which resolved without complications.\nMild eczema in infancy, now resolved and needing no treatment.\nImmunisations up to date, including the pre-school booster.\nHearing and vision checks at nursery were normal.\nHer brother had similar in-toeing at the same age, which resolved without treatment by the age of six.\n\nExamination:\nIn-toeing gait confirmed on observation, symmetrical, foot progression angle approximately 12 degrees internal bilaterally.\nSource identified as bilateral internal tibial torsion — thigh-foot angle internally rotated 10 degrees on each side.\nHips: full and symmetrical internal and external rotation, 45 degrees each way in prone. No excess femoral anteversion.\nFeet structurally normal — no metatarsus adductus, no forefoot adduction, full range of motion at all joints.\nNeurological examination normal with symmetrical reflexes, normal tone and no clonus.\nNo leg-length discrepancy; both 46 cm.\nSpine straight, no sacral dimple.\nHeel strike present, arm swing symmetrical and no toe walking.\nRunning shows the same mild symmetrical in-toeing, with no circumduction.\nHer shoes show even wear on both soles.\nJoint laxity within normal limits for her age; Beighton score 3 out of 9.\nSquats, stands on tiptoe and walks on her heels without difficulty.\n\nAssessment:\nInternal tibial torsion is a normal developmental variant. It is present in most toddlers and unwinds spontaneously with growth, usually resolving by around eight years of age.\nThe tripping is consistent with her age and gait pattern, and her parents report it is already less frequent than a year ago.\nVideo taken by her father on his phone at home was reviewed and matches the clinic findings.\nNo previous podiatry or orthopaedic assessment.\n\nMedication:\nNone.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nDiscussion with the parents:\nExplained that there is good evidence that orthoses, night splints, corrective footwear and exercises do not change the natural history of this condition, and that treating it can create a problem where none exists.\nThey were relieved and accepted the explanation.\nHer father asked whether she should change her current shoes; reassured that any comfortable, well-fitting shoe is fine.\nThey understand that change is gradual and is usually noticed over a year or two rather than weeks.\nWritten information on normal variation in children's walking was given to take home.\nHer mother asked about car parking charges at the clinic; directed to the information desk.\nThey asked whether ballet or other activities would help; told that any activity she enjoys is fine but none is needed as treatment.\nSitting position was discussed; W-sitting is not proven to cause this but she was encouraged to sit cross-legged.\n\nPlan:\nReassurance given. Discharged, with no orthoses, no splints and no exercises.\nSafety-net advice: to return if she develops pain, if the in-toeing becomes asymmetrical, if any skill is lost, or if there is no improvement by around age eight.\nNo further podiatry appointment booked.\nA copy of this letter will be sent to the parents at their request.\nThe family is moving house within the same town in November; the new address will be given to the practice.\nHealth visitor records reviewed with the parents' consent; no previous concerns noted.\n",
      "recipient": "Dr Marcus Lowe, General Practitioner",
      "letterType": "discharge",
      "taskInstruction": "Update the GP and discharge this child following assessment of in-toeing gait, with reassurance and safety-netting."
