      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMiss Chloe Davenport, 16, student.\nStarting A levels at the local sixth-form college in September; lives at home with her mother and stepfather.\nHopes to study dance at a performing arts college after her A levels.\nSeen with her mother, 28 August 2026.\nBooked as a routine appointment at her mother's request.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nPainful plantar verruca beneath the left heel, present for eighteen months.\nFirst noticed in February 2025 as a small rough spot after a term of school swimming lessons.\nInitially painless and treated only with a pumice stone at home.\nNow with mosaic spread to three satellite lesions over the past six months.\nPain on weight-bearing, described as walking on a stone.\nLimiting her dance lessons, which she attends four times weekly and which matter greatly to her.\nShe dances ballet and contemporary and has a graded dance examination in December.\nHas missed two lessons this month because of pain.\nHer dance teacher has allowed her to wear a padded dance sock, which helps a little.\nShe also has a Saturday job in a café, standing for six hours, which she finds painful by the afternoon.\n\nExamination:\nMain lesion 9 mm diameter, well defined, with disruption of the normal skin striae.\nPinpoint capillaries visible on debridement.\nPain on lateral (pinch) pressure rather than on direct pressure — this supports a verruca rather than a corn.\nThree satellite lesions of 3 to 4 mm within 2 cm of the main lesion.\nNo surrounding cellulitis and no secondary infection.\nNo verrucae on the right foot or on the hands.\nSkin otherwise healthy, with no fungal infection between the toes.\nWalks with a slightly shortened stride on the left because of pain.\nLesions photographed today for comparison at the specialist clinic.\n\nGeneral health:\nOtherwise fit and well.\nNo diabetes and no immunosuppression.\nGood circulation — pulses strong, capillary refill under two seconds.\nSensation intact.\nNot pregnant.\nChildhood asthma resolved by the age of ten; no inhaler needed since.\nHeight and weight normal for her age.\nEating and sleeping well; no recent illness.\n\nTreatments already tried:\nOver-the-counter salicylic acid 17 per cent, applied daily for twelve weeks with regular debridement.\nCryotherapy, four sessions at three-week intervals, with no resolution and considerable pain.\nDuct tape occlusion for six weeks.\nShe followed each treatment carefully, as her mother confirmed.\nHer younger brother had a verruca on his toe last year that cleared without treatment.\nEach treatment reduced the size of the main lesion briefly, but it returned within weeks.\nCryotherapy caused blistering after the third session, which healed within ten days.\n\nMedication:\nNone.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nCounselling given:\nExplained that most verrucae clear spontaneously within two years as immunity develops, and that treatment is not always necessary.\nIn this case the duration, the spread and the functional limitation together justify further treatment.\nDiscussed that no single treatment has a high success rate and that a course may be needed.\nShe understands that needling involves a local anaesthetic injection and soreness for a few days afterwards.\nShe is anxious about needles but willing to consider it to get back to full dancing.\nSwims with friends at weekends and already wears a verruca sock.\nHer mother asked whether the verruca could spread to the family; advised not to share towels or footwear.\n\nPlan:\nReferred for consideration of needling under local anaesthetic, or stronger keratolytic therapy with salicylic or monochloroacetic acid, or microwave therapy where available.\nAdvised to continue regular filing and to keep the area covered at the dance studio and swimming pool to reduce transmission.\nWritten information given to take home.\nThe podiatry department contact number is included in the information.\nShe asked about the bus route to the specialist clinic; a timetable was printed for her.\nShe will keep a simple diary of pain and dance attendance to bring to the specialist appointment.\nShe has given her own consent to ongoing care; her mother is supportive and will attend the appointment with her.\nAppointments after 4 pm are preferred so that she does not miss college lessons.\nA copy of this letter has been sent to her general practitioner.\nHer mother would like appointment letters sent to the family email address as well as by post.\nHer college timetable will be confirmed in mid-September, after which she can give firmer availability.\n",
