      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMrs Geraldine Foster, 64, retired school secretary.\nRetired in 2022 after twenty-five years at the same primary school.\nLives with her husband in a semi-detached house with stairs; he is in good health.\nSeen 30 August 2026.\nUnder the community podiatry service since 2019 for routine nail and callus care, seen every eight weeks.\n\nBackground:\nSeropositive rheumatoid arthritis for fifteen years, diagnosed 2011.\nCurrently well controlled systemically; last DAS28 score 2.4.\nUnder rheumatology, reviewed six-monthly.\nHand involvement mild; she manages buttons and jars without difficulty.\nRight carpal tunnel release in 2016, with full recovery.\nLast flare in 2023, settled after a short course of treatment from the rheumatology team.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nIncreasing forefoot pain over about a year, both feet, right worse.\nShe describes the sensation as walking on pebbles.\nNow limiting her to about ten minutes on her feet.\nA year ago she could walk around the town centre for an hour.\nPain scored 7 out of 10 at the end of the day and 3 out of 10 at rest.\nShe has given up her weekly volunteering at a charity shop because of the standing.\nShe now drives short distances she used to walk, such as to the post office.\n\nExamination:\nMarked hallux valgus bilaterally, approximately 40 degrees right and 35 degrees left.\nClawing of the lesser toes with dorsal subluxation at the metatarsophalangeal joints.\nPlantar fat pad displaced distally, leaving the metatarsal heads exposed.\nThick callus over the second and third metatarsal heads on the right.\nAn early pre-ulcerative lesion beneath the right second metatarsal head — macerated callus with underlying haemorrhage, skin still intact.\nBursitis over the dorsum of the second and third toes from shoe pressure.\nNo ulceration and no infection at present.\nLeft forefoot changes similar but less advanced, with no pre-ulcerative lesion.\nAnkle and subtalar joints with good range and no active synovitis.\nRange at the first metatarsophalangeal joints reduced but not painful on movement.\n\nVascular and neurological:\nPedal pulses present bilaterally. Capillary refill under two seconds.\nNeurology grossly intact; monofilament sensation present at all sites.\nSkin otherwise in good condition, with no fissures and no fungal infection.\nFoot temperature equal on both sides, with no hot spots.\nWeight stable; body mass index 24.\n\nFootwear:\nStandard high-street shoes with a narrow toe box and a 4 cm heel.\nThis is a significant contributor and she is reluctant to change, having always dressed smartly for work.\nShe owns one pair of trainers but wears them only for gardening.\nShe agreed to bring her usual shoes to the next appointment for assessment.\nShe is attending her daughter's wedding in the spring and is worried about what she can wear.\n\nMedication:\nMethotrexate 20 mg weekly, taken on Mondays.\nAdalimumab fortnightly.\nFolic acid 5 mg weekly, taken on Thursdays, a different day from the methotrexate.\nOmeprazole 20 mg daily.\n\nAllergies:\nSulfasalazine — rash.\n\nManagement provided today:\nCallus debrided from both metatarsal heads and the pre-ulcerative lesion offloaded.\nSemi-compressed felt padding applied.\nWider footwear advised and the reasoning explained in terms of her ulcer risk rather than fashion.\nShown how to check her soles daily with a mirror; she knows to report any new pain, swelling or broken skin.\nPodiatry review in two weeks to reassess the lesion and renew the padding.\nShe asked whether a blue badge application could be supported; advised to contact the local council.\nThe felt padding should stay dry; she will sponge-wash the foot until the next review.\n\nPlan and concern:\nReferred to the multidisciplinary foot clinic.\nThe deformity is progressing and the pre-ulcerative lesion means ulceration is likely within months without intervention.\nBespoke orthoses with metatarsal offloading and a footwear assessment are needed, and a surgical opinion on forefoot reconstruction should be considered.\nIf surgery is considered, plan perioperative methotrexate and adalimumab with rheumatology.\nShe would consider surgery if it helped her walk further, but would prefer to try orthoses first.\nShe has read about forefoot surgery online and has questions about recovery time.\nHer immunosuppression raises the stakes should an ulcer become infected, and this is noted for the receiving team.\nHer husband drives and can bring her to appointments at any time.\nBowel screening completed in 2025, normal.\nA copy of this letter has been sent to her general practitioner.\nShe is a keen gardener with an allotment, which she now visits less often.\nHer grandchildren visit at weekends, and she would like to be able to walk them to the park again.\n",
