      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMr Terence Boyle, 62.\nLives with his wife; his daughter lives nearby.\nWorks part time as a warehouse supervisor.\nHe and his wife live in a ground-floor flat.\nSeen as an emergency appointment, 1 September 2026, 10.30 am.\nHis wife telephoned the clinic first thing this morning after noticing a smell from the dressing.\n\nDiabetes history:\nType 2 diabetes for sixteen years.\nHbA1c 81 mmol/mol.\nEstablished peripheral neuropathy.\nPrevious ulcer on the same toe in 2024, healed.\nThat ulcer healed in about ten weeks with offloading and dressings, without infection.\nUnder the podiatry high-risk foot service since then, seen every three months; skin intact at his last visit in June 2026.\nDiabetic eye screening up to date.\nAttends the diabetes clinic at his practice twice a year and checks his glucose at home twice daily.\nEx-smoker; stopped in 2010.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nDeteriorating ulcer of the right hallux over three weeks.\nHe first noticed a blister after new shoes and has been dressing it himself.\nThe shoes were bought for a family wedding in August.\nHe used plasters from the chemist and did not seek advice because the toe was not painful.\nHe had not been checking his feet daily, although he had been taught to do so.\nFeels generally unwell for four days, with poor appetite and shivering last night.\nNo vomiting and no chest pain; alert and orientated.\nDrinking fluids normally today.\nHis wife reports he has been more tired than usual and sleeping in the afternoons this week.\n\nWound assessment:\nDeep ulcer at the plantar aspect of the hallux, 15 mm diameter.\nProbes to bone — positive probe-to-bone test using a sterile blunt probe.\nMalodorous with purulent exudate.\nSurrounding erythema extending 3 cm proximally and not blanching.\nThe whole toe is swollen in a sausage shape — dactylitis.\nNo crepitus and no gas in the tissues clinically.\nNo other ulcers; skin on the left foot intact.\nMild callus over the left fifth metatarsal head, unbroken.\n\nSystemic:\nTemperature 38.1 degrees Celsius.\nPulse 96, blood pressure 132/78, respiratory rate 18.\nHe appears unwell.\nOxygen saturation 97 per cent on room air.\nWeight 92 kg, stable over the past year.\n\nNeurovascular:\nNeuropathy present, monofilament absent at all sites.\nPedal pulses palpable but the foot is warm and swollen, which makes assessment difficult.\nLeft pedal pulses palpable; no history of calf pain on walking.\nRight foot clearly warmer than the left on touch.\n\nInvestigations today:\nC-reactive protein 96 mg/L.\nWhite cell count 14.2 x 10^9/L.\nCapillary glucose 16 mmol/L.\nPlain radiograph shows cortical erosion and lucency around the distal phalanx, suggestive of osteomyelitis.\nNo previous radiograph of this foot available for comparison.\nNo superficial wound swab taken, as deep tissue sampling on admission is more useful.\n\nMedication:\nMetformin 1 g twice daily.\nInsulin, recently started, with variable control.\nAtorvastatin 40 mg at night.\nWith sepsis and possible contrast imaging, please withhold metformin on admission and manage glucose with insulin.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nPlan and concern:\nUrgent same-day referral. I telephoned the diabetic foot service at 11.00 am and he is being admitted now.\nHe has been advised to remain non-weight-bearing.\nAs the sepsis screen is positive, an emergency ambulance was called at 11.05 am to take him to hospital.\nHis wife is travelling with him and is bringing his medicines and glucose meter.\nHe understands why admission is needed and agrees with the plan.\nA wheelchair was used to take him to the ambulance.\nThis is a limb-threatening deep infection requiring intravenous antibiotics, MRI and probable surgical debridement, and it meets the criteria for immediate admission rather than outpatient management.\nSepsis screen positive; discussed with the on-call team, who advised immediate attendance.\nBlood cultures and deep tissue sample to be taken on arrival, with IV antibiotics started without delay per local diabetic foot infection guideline; antibiotics are not to be withheld to protect cultures.\nWound photograph taken at 10.45 am and attached to the electronic referral.\nHe asked whether his car could stay in the clinic car park; his son-in-law will collect it later.\nHis daughter has told his employer that he will be absent.\nThe podiatry high-risk foot team will follow him up after discharge.\nA copy of this letter has been sent to his general practitioner.\nHis daughter has his house keys and will collect anything else he needs.\nHe asked whether the ward has a television for the football; his daughter will check.\nReferral letter, medication list and recent clinic notes sent electronically at 11.20 am.\n",
